    "html": "<h2>What feedback clustering actually is</h2>\n<p><strong>Feedback clustering</strong> is the process of grouping raw user comments into repeating themes so the patterns become visible. Instead of re-reading a Slack thread of 80 messages, you see that 22 notes are about Pricing, 14 about Bugs, 9 about UI/UX, and so on. The frequency count turns a pile of notes into a ranked shortlist.</p>\n<p>FeedbackHub applies this directly: you paste feedback lines from Slack, DMs, or surveys, and it returns theme buckets with a count and a suggested build-next pick. It is a practical way to make qualitative feedback countable.</p>\n<h2>Why a pile of notes is not insight</h2>\n<p>A raw export of feedback is not insight — it is a list of moments. The value appears only when you can answer: <em>what do users mention most, and how often?</em> Without clustering, the loudest or most recent voice in the thread tends to win, not the most common one.</p>\n<p>Clustering corrects for that bias by counting. It does not assume the most frequent theme is the most important — only that it is the most said, which is a useful input to a decision you still make.</p>\n<h2>The buckets most feedback falls into</h2>\n<p>A useful default set of themes:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Pricing</strong> — cost, plans, value, willingness to pay.</li><li><strong>Bugs</strong> — broken exports, crashes, errors.</li><li><strong>UI/UX</strong> — design, layout, confusing flows.</li><li><strong>Performance</strong> — speed, load time, lag.</li><li><strong>Other</strong> — anything that does not fit the above.</li></ul>\n<p>Most consumer and B2B feedback maps onto one of these, which keeps the clustering simple and the count readable.</p>\n<h2>What feedback clustering cannot do</h2>\n<p>Feedback clustering reveals emphasis across notes. It does <strong>not</strong> tell you which feature to build, what is best for revenue, or what your strategy should be. Those are judgments that depend on your roadmap, constraints, and context — none of which live in the notes themselves.</p>\n<p>Treat the output as a structured view of what users said, not as a verdict on what to do.</p>\n<h2>How FeedbackHub applies this</h2>\n<p>FeedbackHub takes the lines you already have and returns theme buckets, a frequency count, and a suggested build-next pick. Free is unlimited; Pro at $19/mo adds sentiment and dedupe so repeated points and tone are handled more cleanly.</p>\n<p>The build-next pick is a suggestion derived from frequency — it is decision-support, not a directive. You confirm it against your own plan.</p>\n",
